## Tool versions and updates

Development tools use `~=` constraints in `pyproject.toml`: the tested patch
is the minimum, later patches in the same minor are allowed. Run tools from
this project's environment. Ruff's `required-version` enforces the same range.
Editor extensions may ship their own tools; use the project environment where
supported and run the project command-line checks before committing.

`.womm-template.json` records the generator, template version and original
choices. It is provenance, not a list of the tools currently installed.

{% if use_uv %}The committed `uv.lock` records exact resolved versions, so `uv sync --locked`
keeps installs reproducible. To update a tool within its allowed range, run
`uv lock --upgrade-package TOOL`, then `uv sync --locked --group dev`.
{% else %}To update a tool within its allowed range, run
`python -m pip install --upgrade --group dev`.
{% endif %}Before moving to another minor version, change the dependency constraint and
any matching version guard, review the release notes, and update the
configuration if needed. Black's exact version guard is omitted because it
cannot express a minor range; its dependency constraint sets the bounds.
{% if use_uv %}Run all quality, test and build checks before committing the updated
configuration and lockfile.
{% else %}Reinstall with `python -m pip install -e . --group dev`, then run all
quality, test and build checks. Dependency constraints do not lock transitive
dependencies; use a separate constraints workflow if you need a fully locked
pip environment.
{% endif %}Upgrading WOMM does not update this project's files.

To explicitly update the tooling supported by your installed WOMM release,
run `womm setup --update --dry-run`, then `womm setup --update` and confirm
the plan. Reviewed scalar moves are automatic; block moves and destructive
config migrations need `--allow-destructive-actions`. Unknown version
transitions and conflicting values require manual review, even with that flag.
WOMM validates the target configuration in a temporary copy, backs up changed
files under `.womm-backups/update/` and records successful operations in
`.womm-update.json`. Run the project checks after applying. A failed install
restores the files, but the environment may need to be synchronized again.

